<main id="main">
<section class="phero"><div class="phero-bg"><img src="/assets/img/photos/rowhouse-street.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/rowhouse-street-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/rowhouse-street.webp 1024w" sizes="100vw" width="1024" height="768" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.18"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><ol><li><a href="/">Home</a></li><li aria-current="page">Common questions</li></ol></nav><p class="kicker">22 answers in 5 topics</p><h1>Insurance Appraisal Questions from Maryland Owners</h1><p class="lede">Plain answers about the appraisal process, costs, Maryland deadlines, the MIA and storm, water and winter claims.</p><div class="btns"><a class="btn brass" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn ghost-l" href="/start-a-claim-review/">Request a free review</a></div></div></section><div class="wrap two-col"><article class="prose"><nav class="jump" aria-label="FAQ topics"><a href="#t0">Appraisal basics</a><a href="#t1">Costs &amp; fees</a><a href="#t2">Timing &amp; deadlines in Maryland</a><a href="#t3">Working with your insurer &amp; the MIA</a><a href="#t4">Storm, water &amp; winter claims</a></nav><h2 id="t0">Appraisal basics</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>What is the appraisal clause in a Maryland property policy?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="a"><p>It is a section of most homeowners and commercial property policies that lets either you or your insurer resolve a disagreement over the amount of a covered loss outside of court. Each side names an appraiser, the two appraisers pick an umpire, and an award signed by any two of the three generally sets the amount. The exact wording varies, so read your own policy.</p></div></details><details><summary>Does appraisal decide whether my claim is covered?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="a"><p>Generally, no. Appraisal is designed to decide how much the damage costs, not whether the policy covers it. If your insurer has denied the claim on coverage grounds, talk with a Maryland attorney or consider a complaint to the Maryland Insurance Administration.</p></div></details><details><summary>Is the appraisal award binding?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="a"><p>Most policy forms treat a signed award as setting the amount of loss, and courts generally give awards considerable weight. Awards can sometimes be challenged, for example over fraud, bias or a panel deciding issues outside its authority. Ask an attorney if you are concerned about a specific award.</p></div></details><details><summary>Can my insurer demand appraisal instead of me?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="a"><p>Yes, under most appraisal clauses either party can invoke it. If your insurer sends a demand, you will usually need to name your own appraiser within the time the policy sets. Reading the demand carefully and responding on time protects your position.</p></div></details><details><summary>Is Maryland Insurance Appraiser a public adjuster or law firm?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="a"><p>No. Maryland Insurance Appraiser is an independent appraisal firm that handles appraisal representation, estimate review and umpire work. We don’t give legal advice or negotiate coverage, and we will tell you when a question needs an attorney.</p></div></details></div><h2 id="t1">Costs &amp; fees</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Who pays for the appraisers and the umpire?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="a"><p>Under most policies, each side pays its own appraiser and the umpire’s fee is split equally. Other shared costs, such as an engineer the panel agrees to bring in, are often divided the same way. Check your policy, because some forms word this differently.</p></div></details><details><summary>How are independent appraisers usually paid?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="a"><p>Common arrangements include hourly rates, a flat fee or a fee tied to the size of the claim. Whatever the structure, get it in writing before work starts. Be cautious about any arrangement that could make the appraiser look less than disinterested.</p></div></details><details><summary>Is appraisal worth it for a small claim?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="a"><p>It depends on the gap and the costs. If the difference between estimates is only a few thousand dollars, appraiser and umpire fees may eat most of it. A quick estimate review can help you decide before you commit.</p></div></details><details><summary>Can I recover my appraisal costs from the insurer?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="a"><p>Appraisal costs are usually your responsibility under the policy, even if the award is higher than the insurer’s estimate. Whether any expenses can be recovered in a separate legal claim is a question for a Maryland attorney.</p></div></details></div><h2 id="t2">Timing &amp; deadlines in Maryland</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>How long does an appraisal take?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="a"><p>Many residential appraisals wrap up within a few weeks to a few months, depending on how quickly appraisers are named, how complex the damage is and whether the umpire is needed. Large commercial or condominium claims can take longer. Good documentation is the biggest factor you control.</p></div></details><details><summary>How soon must I name my appraiser?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="a"><p>Many policies give each side about 20 days after the written demand. Your policy may use a different period, so check the wording and calendar the date as soon as a demand is sent or received.</p></div></details><details><summary>Does appraisal pause the deadline to sue my insurer?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="a"><p>Don’t assume so. Maryland’s general contract limitations period is three years, and Maryland law generally doesn’t let property policies shorten it, but when that clock starts can be disputed. Ask an attorney about your dates before relying on appraisal to protect them.</p></div></details><details><summary>Is there a deadline to invoke appraisal?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="a"><p>Some policies don’t set one, while others tie appraisal to other claim steps. Waiting too long or acting inconsistently with appraisal can create arguments that the right was waived. It is usually best to decide once the insurer’s final estimate is clear.</p></div></details><details><summary>When is recoverable depreciation paid after an award?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="a"><p>Usually after repairs are completed and documented. Many policies set a time window for completing repairs to collect replacement cost, so read that section and keep invoices and photos of the finished work.</p></div></details></div><h2 id="t3">Working with your insurer &amp; the MIA</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Should I file a complaint with the Maryland Insurance Administration or demand appraisal?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="a"><p>They do different jobs. An MIA complaint addresses how the insurer handled your claim, such as delays or unexplained decisions, while appraisal sets the dollar amount of a covered loss. Some people use both; an attorney can help if legal remedies are involved.</p></div></details><details><summary>Can I keep talking to my adjuster during appraisal?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="a"><p>Yes, and you should keep communication professional and in writing where possible. Your appraiser handles the valuation discussion with the insurer’s appraiser, but the adjuster still manages payments, coverage letters and other claim steps.</p></div></details><details><summary>What if my insurer refuses to take part in appraisal?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="a"><p>If the policy contains an appraisal clause, refusal raises legal questions about enforcing the contract. Many policies allow a court to appoint an umpire, and courts can be asked to compel appraisal in some situations. That is a matter for a Maryland attorney.</p></div></details><details><summary>What if I’m insured through the Maryland Joint Insurance Association?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="a"><p>MJIA policies have their own policy forms, so read the conditions section to see what dispute resolution options apply. The same basic principles about documenting the loss and comparing estimates still help.</p></div></details></div><h2 id="t4">Storm, water &amp; winter claims</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Can appraisal resolve a hurricane deductible dispute?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="a"><p>Appraisal can set the amount of damage, but whether a percentage hurricane or named-storm deductible was triggered is usually a coverage question about policy definitions. If the trigger is disputed, ask an attorney or the MIA alongside any appraisal of the amount.</p></div></details><details><summary>Does appraisal help with ice dam claims in Western Maryland?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="a"><p>It can, when the insurer accepts that interior water damage from an ice dam is covered but undervalues the repair. Disputes over whether long-term seepage or wear is excluded are coverage questions rather than appraisal questions.</p></div></details><details><summary>My insurer says the storm damage is old wear and tear. Can appraisal fix that?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="a"><p>Whether damage was caused by the storm is sometimes treated as part of the amount of loss and sometimes as coverage, depending on the policy and the facts. Because Maryland law on this point can turn on details, get legal advice if causation is the main dispute.</p></div></details><details><summary>Is flood damage handled through my homeowners appraisal clause?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="a"><p>Usually not. Standard homeowners policies typically exclude flood, and flood insurance through the NFIP or a private carrier has its own dispute procedures. Tidal or stream flooding claims should be checked against the separate flood policy.</p></div></details></div></article><aside class="side"><div class="side-in"><div class="box box-call"><h2 class="box-h">Talk to an appraiser</h2><p>A free first conversation about your estimate — and a straight answer on whether appraisal fits.</p><a class="box-tel" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg>(844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn brass block" href="/start-a-claim-review/">Send claim details</a><small>Mon–Fri, 8am–6pm ET</small></div><div class="box"><h2 class="box-h">Resources</h2><ul class="box-list"><li><a href="/the-appraisal-process/">The appraisal process<svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M9 18l6-6-6-6"/></svg></a></li><li><a href="/common-questions/" aria-current="page">Common questions<svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M9 18l6-6-6-6"/></svg></a></li><li><a href="/appraisal-glossary/">Appraisal glossary<svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M9 18l6-6-6-6"/></svg></a></li><li><a href="/knowledge-base/">Knowledge base<svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M9 18l6-6-6-6"/></svg></a></li></ul></div></div></aside></div><section class="strip"><div class="wrap strip-in rv"><div><h2>Didn’t see your question?</h2><p>Call or email — we’re glad to talk it through.</p></div><div class="btns"><a class="btn brass" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn ghost-l" href="/start-a-claim-review/">Email the estimate</a></div></div></section>
